fact WorkItem(id: "WI-979", description: some(value: "FOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-975 (see docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md).\n`sort X ... end` that REUSES an existing symbol never records the Sort category,\nso a `namespace X` written BEFORE the sort silently breaks the sort.\n\nMEASURED, one pair, the two declarations in both orders:\n\n  namespace q4a                     namespace q4b\n    namespace Rec                     sort Rec\n      operation helper(...)             entity Rec(n: Int64)\n    end                               end\n    sort Rec                          namespace Rec\n      entity Rec(n: Int64)              operation helper(...)\n    end                               end\n  end                               end\n\n  ns-first:   kind_of=Some(Namespace)  has_kind(Sort)=FALSE\n              has_kind(Entity)=FALSE   nested `q4a.Rec.Rec` EXISTS\n  sort-first: kind_of=Some(Sort)       has_kind(Sort)=true\n              has_kind(Entity)=true    nested `q4b.Rec.Rec` absent\n\nSo in ns-first order the name is not a Sort, `is_sort_scope` is therefore false,\nthe EPONYMOUS CONSTRUCTOR never collapses (§6.3 / WI-926), and a phantom nested\n`X.X` is defined. Downstream, a member call on such a receiver reports the sort\nas `q3b.Rec.Rec` and dot dispatch misses.\n\nCAUSE, in `scan_items_pass1`'s `Item::SortWithBody` arm (kb/load.rs ~2700):\n\n    let (sym, is_new) = if let Some(&existing) = kb.symbols.by_qualified_name.get(&qualified) {\n        (existing, false)                      // <-- no add_kind(Sort) here\n    } else {\n        (kb.symbols.define(&short, &qualified, SymbolKind::Sort, ...), true)\n    };\n\nThis is EXACTLY the defect WI-926 fixed one arm below. The `Item::Entity` arm\nrecords its category explicitly, with a comment saying why: \"Recorded from the\nDECLARATION, not from which arm above produced the symbol -- two of the three\nnever call `define`, so relying on `define` to carry the category silently loses\nit.\" `SortWithBody` has the same two-arm shape and was never given the same fix.\n\nNOTE the reuse branch is also the PRELUDE-bootstrap path (`register_prelude`\ndefines `anthill.prelude.List` before stdlib's `sort List ... end` reuses it) --\nthat one is unaffected because `register_prelude` already defines with Sort, so\nthe fix is additive there.\n\nRELATED, decide together: `SortWithBody`'s reuse branch also skips the\n`add_parent` that wires the sort's scope to its enclosing scope, which is why\n`entity X(...)` followed by `sort X ... end` produces a body that resolves\nNOTHING from outside itself. Proposal 059 R1 refuses that shape outright, so\nthat half may become unreachable rather than fixed -- but the category loss\nabove is reachable via `namespace X` + `sort X`, which R2 explicitly BLESSES.\n\nACCEPTANCE: both declaration orders give the same categories and the same\neponymous collapse. A test asserts, for the pair above, `has_kind(Sort)` and\n`has_kind(Entity)` true in BOTH orders and no nested `X.X` in either, and drives\na member call on the receiver in BOTH orders. Say at the test site which\nassertions fail when the fix is backed out (the ns-first row does; sort-first\npasses either way, by design, as the control). cargo-test green via\nrustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T13:42:46Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-979", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (2026-08-04). ONE LINE SHIPPED, AND ONE REVERTED — the revert is the\nmore useful half of this ticket.\n\nWHAT LANDED. `add_kind(sym, SymbolKind::Sort)` unconditionally after the two-arm\nlookup in scan_items_pass1's Item::SortWithBody arm. `define` accumulates\ncategories (WI-926) but the reuse arm never calls it, so the `sort` keyword's own\ncategory was lost whenever the name already existed. Exactly the fix the\nItem::Entity arm already carries, for the same two-arm shape; idempotent, so the\nprelude-bootstrap path is unaffected.\n\nWHAT I BUILT, MEASURED GREEN, AND THEN REVERTED. Driving found a second\n`is_new` gate on the same arm — the variant-exposure link — and I un-gated it,\nadded an idempotent `add_parent_once`, and ran the full workspace suite: 0\nfailures. /code-review then refuted it three ways, each of which I re-drove myself\nbefore acting:\n\n  1. `is_new` is false for TWO populations, and un-gating serves the wrong one.\n     register_prelude pre-registers anthill.prelude.{List, Option, TypeExtractor,\n     EffectExpression} and anthill.reflect.{MemberKind, Expr, Pattern}, all\n     re-declared with variants in stdlib source, so all seven gained an exposure\n     link they never had — ~55 short names newly bare-resolvable. DRIVEN\n     consequence: bare `guarded` under wildcard imports goes from resolving\n     uniquely to LogicalQuery.guarded to AMBIGUOUS against\n     EffectExpression.guarded. Both directions measured by flipping the one line.\n  2. It silently half-wired the shape this ticket deliberately left refused.\n     `entity Rec(n)` + `sort Rec { entity Inner }` started loading clean with bare\n     `Inner` resolving OUT, while the body still could not see IN (the enclosing\n     link stays gated). And MY OWN PINNING TEST FOR THAT SHAPE COULD NOT SEE IT —\n     its fixture had no variant, so `has_variant` was false and the new path was\n     never entered. A pinning test blind to the change it pins is not a pin; the\n     fixture now carries a variant.\n  3. My justification for `add_parent_once` was FALSE. I wrote that a duplicate\n     parent link 'can turn a unique resolution into Ambiguous'. It cannot:\n     resolve_in_scope_recursive has a `visited` guard (intern.rs:672) and dedups\n     matches (723-725), and a genuinely duplicated identical link (via a plain\n     `import <ns>.C` beside `sort C { entity Red }`) loads clean. The helper was\n     harmless and existed to prevent a harm that does not occur.\n\nTHE LESSON, and it is the one this ticket should be remembered for: A GREEN FULL\nSUITE WAS NOT EVIDENCE. There is exactly one wildcard import in the whole repo,\ninside a parse test, so nothing in-tree could trip the ambiguity. I had flagged\nthis exact risk to myself before making the change ('could ADD parent links for\nevery prelude sort ... changing resolution globally') and then accepted 2900+\npassing tests in place of measuring it. The corpus does not exercise a resolution\nroute that no file uses.\n\nThe reverted half is WI-985, with both driven consequences, the reason `if\nhas_variant` alone is wrong, and an acceptance clause requiring its own\nambiguity guard — because the suite will be green for the naive fix again.\n\nALSO NOTED, not fixed: `logical_sort_qn`'s guard doc says '`app.Config` is a\nNamespace' as if exclusive. Since this ticket a symbol can carry BOTH Namespace\nand Sort, so the guard passes where it used to fail. It takes three same-named\ndeclarations to reach and no such shape is in the corpus, so it is a noted premise\nrather than a measured wrong answer — recorded at the site.\n\nTESTS: wi979_declaration_order_test (5). Categories in both orders; no phantom\n`X.X`; a DRIVEN member call on a receiver built from the main entry's constructor\n(asserting the value, not that the file loaded); the variant-exposure defect\npinned in both directions with expect_load_errors, so WI-985 cannot land silently;\nand the `entity X` + `sort X` shape pinned as still failing loudly, its fixture\nnow carrying a variant. CONTROLS said at each site: every sort-first row passes\nwith the fix backed out. Backing out `add_kind` fails 4 of 5.\n\nA MISREAD WORTH RECORDING. On the first run all three tests failed and I read the\nmessage as the CONTROL failing, because both fixtures put `drive()` on line 10 and\nthe error was `10:32` either way. I chased it as a fixture problem through seven\nprobe variants — every one loading clean — before noticing the coincidence. It was\nthe ns-first row all along. Separately I nearly pinned an incomplete error list\nbecause my probe printed only the first two of three errors.\n\nFull workspace green via scripts/test.sh, EXIT=0, zero failures.\n\n059 UPDATED: its 'Today that is not quite true — a secondary entry read before the\nmain one breaks the sort (WI-979)' caveat is now true for the CLASSIFICATION and\nthe eponymous collapse (variant visibility is WI-985, and the note says so); and\nits problem table said `entity X` then `sort X { … }` 'loads', where it is\nrefused with three unresolved-name errors — driven.", at: "2026-08-04T13:42:50Z")
